  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThe broad upper-level trough over the Pacific Northwest \ncontinues to govern our weather pattern. Moisture streaming into\nthe region remains focused primarily across the Central \nMountains. High-resolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) \ncontinue to indicate multiple rounds of showers and embedded \nthunderstorms traversing the higher terrain today. Given the \nconvective nature of the precipitation and the potential for \nbrief, heavy downpours, a localized Flash Flood Watch has been \nissued for the Wapiti burn scar near Stanley. Hydrophobic soils \nwithin the burn perimeter make this area highly susceptible to \nrapid runoff and debris flows, even with modest rainfall rates.\n\nElsewhere across eastern Idaho, conditions will remain largely \ndry. Winds across the Snake Plain, Magic Valley, and Arco Desert\nwill remain breezy out of the west-southwest this afternoon, \nbut peak gusts are expected to be a few notches lower than \nWednesday's near-advisory levels. High temperatures today will \nremain cooler than normal, holding in the 60s and 70s but a few \ndegrees warmer across the Snake Plain and Magic Valley compared \nto yesterday. \n\nAs the initial waves of energy lift out of the region, we will \nsee a brief reprieve on Friday and Saturday. Precipitation \ncoverage will wane, and a subtle warming trend will take hold. \nExpect afternoon maximum temperatures to increase slightly each\nday, pushing lower-elevation valleys back into the upper 70s \nand lower 80s by Saturday afternoon. Breezy afternoon winds will\npersist, but overall weather impacts will be minimal ahead of \nour next major system.\n\nConfidence continues to increase regarding a significant pattern\nshift for the latter half of the holiday weekend. Global model\nensembles are in excellent agreement that the parent upper-level\nlow anchored off the Pacific Northwest coast will finally eject\neastward, digging directly into the Intermountain West on Sunday\nand Monday. This system will bring a surge of strong atmospheric\nlift and deep moisture, resulting in high probabilities for\nwidespread wetting rains across all of eastern Idaho. Still some\nuncertainty with respect to precip amounts, which have trended\nback down, but high confidence in below normal temps on the\nLabor Day holiday with many locations likely staying in the 60s.",
